## The idea

Keep the intervals **merged and sorted** in a SortedSet, and keep the total covered length in a field that we update on every change. Adding an interval: find the interval just before it (it may overlap or touch), then swallow every stored interval that starts inside the new range, and insert one merged interval. Because the total is maintained as we go, GetCoveredSize is just reading a number.

## Step by step

1. Ignore empty or reversed intervals (end ≤ start).
2. Start with lo = start, hi = end.
3. Look at the stored interval with the largest start ≤ start. If its end ≥ start, it overlaps or touches: extend lo/hi to include it and remove it (subtract its length from the total).
4. Then repeatedly take the first stored interval whose start is between lo and hi: extend hi to its end if needed, remove it, subtract its length.
5. Insert [lo, hi) and add its length to the total.
6. GetCoveredSize returns the total — O(1).

## Worked example

**Input:** Add [1,5), Add [10,12), Add [3,11)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Add [1,5) | set {[1,5)}; total 4 |
| Add [10,12) | nothing before it overlaps → set {[1,5), [10,12)}; total 6 |
| Add [3,11) | interval before 3 is [1,5): 5 ≥ 3 → merge → lo 1, hi 11; remove it (total 2) |
| swallow | [10,12) starts inside [1,11] → hi = 12; remove it (total 0) |
| insert | [1,12) → total 11 |

**Result:** Covered size 11

## Cost

AddInterval: O(log n) amortised — each interval is inserted once and removed at most once. GetCoveredSize: O(1). Memory O(n).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// Half-open intervals [start, end): length = end - start, and touching intervals merge.
public class CoveredIntervals {
    // Disjoint, merged intervals, sorted by Start.
    private readonly SortedSet<(int Start, int End)> _set = new SortedSet<(int Start, int End)>();
    private long _covered = 0;          // kept up to date on every add/remove — never recomputed

    public long GetCoveredSize() {
        return _covered;
    }

    public void AddInterval(int start, int end) {
        if (end <= start) return;       // empty or reversed: nothing to add

        int lo = start, hi = end;

        // 1. The stored interval that starts at or before `start` (the closest one).
        if (_set.Count > 0 && _set.Min.Start <= start) {
            // all intervals with Start <= start; their Max is the closest one
            (int Start, int End) before = _set.GetViewBetween(_set.Min, (start, int.MaxValue)).Max;
            if (before.End >= start) {              // overlaps or touches: merge it in
                lo = before.Start;
                if (before.End > hi) hi = before.End;
                RemoveInterval(before);
            }
        }

        // 2. Swallow every stored interval that starts inside [lo, hi].
        while (true) {
            SortedSet<(int Start, int End)> inside = _set.GetViewBetween((lo, int.MinValue), (hi, int.MaxValue));
            // Take the first interval in the view, if there is one. (Do NOT use inside.Count here:
            // on a view, Count walks every element in it, which makes big merges slow.)
            bool found = false;
            (int Start, int End) next = (0, 0);
            foreach ((int Start, int End) interval in inside) { next = interval; found = true; break; }
            if (!found) break;
            if (next.End > hi) hi = next.End;
            RemoveInterval(next);
        }

        // 3. Insert the merged interval.
        _set.Add((lo, hi));
        _covered += hi - lo;
    }

    private void RemoveInterval((int Start, int End) interval) {
        _set.Remove(interval);
        _covered -= interval.End - interval.Start;
    }
}
```

